# Docker / Kubernetes / Terraform coding style

Narrows `core-common-rules`' stack-agnostic style rules to the file shapes
this pack owns: Dockerfiles, Docker Compose files, Kubernetes/Helm YAML, and
Terraform. Applies only to those file types — not to application source code
under any of these directories.

## Dockerfile

- Order instructions from least to most frequently changing (base image,
  system packages, dependency manifests, dependency install, then source
  copy) so Docker's build cache is invalidated only by the layer that
  actually changed, not by every layer after a source edit.
- Use multi-stage builds (`FROM ... AS builder`, then a slim final stage
  that copies only the built artifact) for any compiled or bundled
  language — the build toolchain never belongs in the shipped image.
- Prefer a minimal, actively maintained base image (`-slim`/`-alpine`, or a
  distroless/hardened image) over a full OS image unless the project
  genuinely needs the extra tooling at runtime.
- One logical concern per `RUN` line where it aids readability, but chain
  related package-manager commands (`apt-get update && apt-get install
  -y ... && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*`) into a single `RUN` so the
  package cache does not persist as a separate layer.
- Ship a `.dockerignore` alongside the Dockerfile that excludes `.git/`,
  `node_modules/`/`vendor/` (rebuilt inside the image), and any local
  env/secret file — an unfiltered build context both bloats the image and
  risks copying something that should never leave the host.

## Docker Compose

- Pin every service's `image:` the same way a Dockerfile's `FROM` is
  pinned (a tag, or a digest for anything security-sensitive) — never
  `image: latest` in a committed compose file.
- Keep secrets and per-environment values in `.env`/`environment:` sourced
  from the shell or a secrets file that is itself gitignored, not
  hardcoded into `docker-compose.yml`.
- Name every custom network and volume explicitly rather than relying on
  Compose's default naming, so multi-project setups do not collide.

## Kubernetes and Helm

- One resource kind's concerns per manifest file where the project's
  layout allows it (a Deployment, its Service, its ConfigMap as separate
  files or a clearly delimited multi-document YAML) rather than one
  sprawling file mixing unrelated workloads.
- Set explicit `resources.requests`/`resources.limits` on every container
  — an unbounded container can starve its node's other workloads, and the
  scheduler cannot place it sensibly without a request.
- Use a Helm `values.yaml` (or Kustomize overlay) for anything that varies
  per environment — a hardcoded namespace, hostname, or replica count
  inside a template defeats the point of templating.
- Label and select consistently (`app.kubernetes.io/name`,
  `app.kubernetes.io/instance`) so Services, NetworkPolicies, and
  `kubectl` tooling can all select the same workload by the same labels.

## Terraform

- Organize a module around `main.tf`/`variables.tf`/`outputs.tf` (plus
  `versions.tf` pinning the provider and Terraform version) rather than
  one large file mixing resource definitions with variable declarations.
- Name resources and variables for what they are (`aws_db_instance.orders`,
  not `aws_db_instance.db1`), and give every variable a `description` and,
  where one exists, a `type` constraint — an untyped variable defers a
  typo to `apply` time instead of `plan`/`validate` time.
- Prefer a module for any resource group reused across environments
  (`modules/network`, `modules/service`) over copy-pasted `.tf` blocks per
  environment directory.
